  "reasoning": "The patient presents with persistent shivering/chills, a very high fever, and a rash localized to the arms and neck. These symptoms form a classic clinical picture for an acute systemic viral infection. Among the provided options, Dengue is strongly characterized by sudden onset of high fever, rigors (shivering), and a maculopapular rash that frequently appears on the extremities (arms) and neck/trunk. Malaria also causes high fever and chills but typically lacks a prominent rash. Chicken pox presents with a vesicular, intensely itchy rash that usually begins on the face and trunk. Impetigo involves honey-crusted sores primarily around the nose and mouth. Drug reactions may cause rashes but do not typically present with severe rigors and high fever as primary features. Therefore, the combination of high fever, shivering, and specific rash distribution most accurately supports Dengue.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
